# identifier-hazard sweep: generation outcome per (name-position × hazardous identifier) — a
# SCORECARD, not a contract. A committed PANIC is a TRACKED-KNOWN gap (today: rule/group names
# whose camel-cased form is a reserved Rust type name or a CDDL keyword hit the `assert!` guards
# in `RustIdent::new`, intermediate.rs:1146/1152 — a deliberate reservation, but via panic
# rather than a graceful `record_rejection`; candidate fix, not blessed-correct). A NEW panic —
# a cell FLIPPING to PANIC, or a panic decaying to a silently-wrong `ok` — is a regression:
# hazardous names must reject gracefully, never `panic!`/`assert!`. `ok` is generate-only — a
# rule/group name that generates but does NOT compile (the r/w generic collision) still records
# `ok`; the compile verdict is the `identifier_hazard_crates_compile` gate (full tier).
# Source: src/tests/identifier_hazard_tests.rs.

